# wtp.list.example - the pattern for wtp.list.
#
# Copy this file to 'wtp.list', in the same folder, and edit it. wtp.bat opens
# one Windows Terminal tab per line below, each sitting in the folder its line
# names, with the global senv.bat and then the senv.bat of that folder already
# run. Without a wtp.list, wtp.bat opens nothing and points back here.
#
# Format:  <folder path>
#      or  <folder path>|<tab title>
#      or  --- <window name>
#
# A line with no title leaves the tab title to whatever runs in the tab, so a
# tool that names its own tab keeps control of it. A line starting with '---'
# closes the window gathered so far and opens a new one; the text after the
# dashes names that window in the wtp.bat log lines, and holds the block
# together when a later run reopens a tab closed by hand. Blank lines and lines
# starting with '#' are ignored, leading and trailing spaces are dropped, and a
# tab character is not a separator: use spaces.
#
# The same folder can be listed several times, once per tab wanted on it.
#
# The two blocks below open one window with three tabs on one project and one
# window with two tabs on another. Replace the paths with your own.

--- main

C:\Users\me\git\myproject
C:\Users\me\git\myproject
C:\Users\me\git\myproject|myproject

--- side

C:\Users\me\git\otherproject
C:\Users\me\git\otherproject|otherproject
